  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 041: Compilers and kernels turn model calculations into chip instructions, so software helps determine how much work the hardware actually completes.

Lesson 042: A faster accelerator saves money only when the time it saves outweighs its higher hourly price.

Lesson 043: Scale-up tightly connects a group of accelerators, while scale-out joins servers and racks into a larger cluster.

Lesson 044: Collective operations let accelerators exchange results, and slow exchanges can leave otherwise fast chips waiting.

Lesson 045: Network interfaces move data into and out of servers, while switches direct it through connections that may be shared.

The big idea

Lesson 045 followed data through the network. Now focus on the connection between two pieces of hardware.

Copper carries electrical signals. Optical fiber carries light. Either can carry the same advertised data rate; the difference is often how far it can do so reliably, and at what power and cost.

A short copper connection can be simple and efficient. Over a longer distance, its signal may become too distorted to read. Fiber can solve that distance problem, but requires equipment to convert electricity into light and back.

1. What happens inside a copper link?

A transmitter sends changing voltages and currents along copper conductors. The receiver reads those changes to recover the data.

A symbol is one distinguishable signal state; depending on the encoding, it can represent one or more bits. A lane is one signaling path. A fast link can combine several lanes.

As the signal travels, it gets weaker and less clear:

- Resistance in the conductor and losses in its insulating material weaken the signal, especially its higher-frequency components.
- Crosstalk means a nearby conductor's signal interferes with this one.
- Connectors and changes in the path's electrical properties can reflect some signal back.
- Noise and temperature changes can make the remaining signal harder to distinguish.

Higher data rates per lane and longer paths generally make these problems harder.

Transmitters can reshape the signal before sending it; this is called pre-emphasis. Receivers can compensate for some distortion; this is called equalization. Both help, but only within limits.

2. Passive and active copper

A passive direct-attach copper cable, or DAC, connects the endpoints without powered signal-processing electronics inside the cable assembly. The endpoints still consume power.

An active copper cable adds electronics to improve the signal. Depending on the design, it can support a longer connection, but adds power, heat and complexity.

The key question is qualified reach: the distance over which a particular cable and hardware combination has been shown to work reliably. Physically reaching the other rack is not enough.

Engineers check the complete channel, meaning the whole signal path. Circuit-board traces—the copper tracks on boards—and connectors use up some of the signal's tolerance for loss before it even travels along the cable.

3. What changes with fiber?

The data starts electrically at the network interface or switch. An optical transmitter converts it into variations in light. Fiber guides that light to the other end. A photodetector senses it and converts it back into an electrical signal.

Fiber loses much less signal over typical data-center distances and does not pick up electrical crosstalk. Thin, light fibers can also be easier to route through crowded cable trays than large copper bundles.

